X:1
T:Mīkhāhī labu?
T:Do you want hot beetroot? (Persian folk song)
C:traditional (Iran), collected by Blair Fairchild
O:Iran (Persia)
R:Persian folk song (comic song)
S:Blair Fairchild (coll. and arr.), Twelve Persian Folk-Songs (London: Novello, 1904), no. 4, pp. 15-17; English words by Alma Strettell; University of Rochester, Sibley Music Library scan
N:A teasing song: a suitor asks "Do you want me to bring you hot beetroot?" (labu, the cooked beetroot sold by street-sellers in the Persian winter), and everyone answers with the loud refrain "nā-nā-ī". Fairchild: "The melodies themselves were written down as faithfully as possible just as they were sung to me in Persia." The notes E F G A B C, ending on E, are close to the dastgah Shur, where a Persian singer may make the F a little flat (koron); Fairchild wrote F natural, and it is kept so. Here the class sings only the first line, twice, with the refrain: the second line of verse 1 (a joke about bringing home a second wife) and Fairchild's verses 2 to 4 (a bedroom joke and two verses about cigarettes) are left out. For that, bar 3 is sung to the words of bar 1, without Fairchild's slur on its first two notes. The refrain's last E is held over a further bar, as printed after verse 2.
M:4/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=88
K:Am
